course: dictionary
lesson: 4121
title: One word, two kinds of glare
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word glare can mean an angry look or a harsh, bright light. «The teacher glared at the noisy class» is about a face full of anger, not about light.
explain: «The dark fur round a meerkat's eyes cuts down glare from the bright desert sun» is different. Here glare is the dazzling light itself, the kind that makes your eyes squint. The words bright, sun and cuts down are the clues.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does «The teacher shone a bright light at the noisy class» make sense? No. So the first meaning is the right one there. Always test your choice before you trust it.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A word can have more than one meaning, so check which one fits the sentence.
fact: «Glare» as a verb means to look at someone in an angry way for a while.
fact: «Glare» as a noun can mean an angry look, or a strong, bright light that hurts the eyes.
fact: Sunglasses reduce glare, and some animals have dark markings that may cut down glare from the sun.
exercise: «The teacher glared at the noisy class.» What does glared mean here? | Walked out of the room quietly | Clapped to show they were good | *Looked at them with an angry face | Shone a bright light on them
exercise: Some cheetahs have dark tear lines from eyes to mouth. What might these lines do? | *They may cut the glare of the sun | They help the cheetah swim faster | They keep the cheetah's eyes wet | They help the cheetah hide at night
exercise: In «The dark fur round a meerkat's eyes cuts down glare from the bright desert sun», what is the glare coming from? | The desert sand at night | *The bright desert sun | The meerkat's eyes | The dark fur
test: «Then it seems every hole has a hard-eyed face glaring at him.» What does glaring show about the faces? | *Anger or hostility | Hunger for food | Tiredness after a long walk | Laughter and play
test: Mia says, «She was in the glare of publicity.» What does glare of publicity mean? | Shining so brightly that she could not see | *With everyone watching her | In a very dark room | Whispering a secret to a friend
exam: Sunglasses help you because they reduce what from the Sun? | noise | *glare | sand | rain
